= How to Play

An essential, spoiler-free primer for Mass Effect players of all
experience levels. Covers the fundamentals you need to survive and
thrive from Eden Prime to the final battle.

== Getting Started

*Difficulty selection.* Normal is recommended for your first playthrough.
It provides a balanced challenge without punishing mistakes. You can
change difficulty at any time from the Options menu if you find combat
too easy or too hard.

*Save habits.* Save often and use multiple save slots. Mass Effect lets
you create manual saves at nearly any time. Rotate through at least
three save slots so you can always go back if a decision does not go
the way you intended. The game also auto-saves at key checkpoints,
but do not rely on auto-saves alone.

*The conversation wheel.* Dialogue in Mass Effect uses a radial wheel.
The left-side options (top to bottom) are generally: investigate (ask
follow-up questions), Paragon (compassionate, diplomatic), and neutral.
The right-side options are: Renegade (aggressive, pragmatic) at the
bottom-right, with other options above. The position of options on the
wheel is consistent -- once you learn the layout, you can navigate
conversations quickly without reading every choice.

== Class Overview

You choose one of six classes at character creation. Your class
determines which weapons, powers, and armor you can use. You cannot
change your class after starting the game (without mods).

#callout("tip")[
  *Soldier (recommended for beginners).* Pure combat. Heavy armor,
  all weapon types, no tech or biotic powers. You rely on gunplay
  and survivability. Straightforward and forgiving -- a great choice
  for learning the combat system.
]

*Infiltrator.* Tech and combat hybrid. Sniper rifles and tech powers
like Overload and Sabotage. The Tactical Cloak ability lets you turn
invisible for repositioning or a devastating sniper shot. High damage
output but requires good aim and positioning.

*Adept.* Pure biotic. Adepts use dark energy powers like Singularity,
Warp, and Throw to control crowds and deal massive combo damage. Very
fragile with light armor only. Rewards mastery of the power combo system.

*Engineer.* Pure tech. Engineers deploy drones, hack enemy synthetics,
and debuff foes with powers like Overload and Incinerate. Light armor
and limited weapons, but you have an answer for every enemy type.

*Vanguard.* Biotic and combat hybrid. Vanguards combine shotguns with
aggressive biotic powers, most notably the Charge ability that launches
you directly at enemies. High risk, high reward. Exciting but punishing
if you charge into a bad situation.

*Sentinel.* Tech and biotic hybrid. Sentinels have access to both tech
and biotic powers, plus the Tech Armor ability that provides damage
resistance. The most versatile class, with tools for every situation.
A great pick if you want to experiment with both power types.

== Combat Basics

*The cover system.* Press the cover button near waist-high objects or
walls to take cover. While in cover, you are protected from most
incoming fire. You can pop out to shoot, aim powers, or direct
squadmates. Move along cover by pressing left or right. Leave cover
by moving away or pressing the cover button again.

*Weapons.* Each weapon type has an effective range:
- *Assault rifles*: medium range, high rate of fire, versatile.
- *Sniper rifles*: long range, high damage per shot, slow firing.
- *Shotguns*: short range, massive close-up damage.
- *Pistols*: short to medium range, reliable backup weapon.
- *Submachine guns*: short range, fast fire rate, lower damage.

Ammunition uses thermal clips. When a weapon's thermal clip is
depleted, you reload a new one. Ammo is plentiful -- do not hoard it.

*Powers.* Each class (and squadmate) has unique powers like Overload,
Singularity, or Concussive Shot. Powers have cooldown timers. After
using a power, you must wait for it to recharge before using it again.
Weapon weight affects power cooldown speed -- carrying heavy weapons
slows your power recharge rate in Mass Effect 3.

*Squad commands.* You can direct your two squadmates to move to a
position or use a specific power on a specific enemy. Open the power
wheel (hold the designated button) to pause combat and issue commands.
Use your squadmates' powers to strip enemy shields (Overload),
lift enemies out of cover (Pull, Singularity), or debuff tough foes
(Warp).

*Health, shields, barriers, and armor.* Enemies (and you) have layers
of protection:
- *Health*: the red bar. Last line of defense.
- *Shields*: blue bar. Tech powers and rapid-fire weapons strip shields
  quickly.
- *Barriers*: purple bar. Biotic defenses. Warp and rapid fire are
  effective.
- *Armor*: yellow bar. Heavy protection. Incendiary ammo, Warp, and
  heavy weapons break armor.

Match your attacks to the defense type. Overload shreds shields but
does little to armor. Incendiary ammo melts armor but glances off
shields.

*Medi-gel and revival.* Medi-gel restores a small amount of health
and can revive fallen squadmates. You carry a limited number of
medi-gel charges. Use them when you or a squadmate goes down, but
try not to waste them on minor damage.

== Squad Management

You bring two squadmates on most missions. Choose them to complement
your class and to handle enemy types your Shepard struggles with.

*Complementing your class.* A Soldier Shepard lacks tech and biotic
abilities -- bring an Engineer or Adept squadmate to cover those gaps.
An Adept Shepard is fragile -- bring a tanky squadmate like a Soldier
or Sentinel to draw fire.

*Talk to everyone.* Between missions, explore your ship and hub areas
and talk to your squadmates. Companions have extensive dialogue trees,
personal stories, and loyalty missions. These conversations are not
optional flavor -- they are some of the best content in the trilogy.
Missing them means missing character development, and in some cases,
critical mission outcomes.

== Morality System

Mass Effect uses a binary morality system: Paragon (compassion,
diplomacy, idealism) and Renegade (pragmatism, intimidation, results
at any cost).

*How it works.* Most conversations offer Paragon (top-right) and
Renegade (bottom-right) responses alongside neutral options.
Accumulating Paragon or Renegade points unlocks Charm (Paragon) or
Intimidate (Renegade) dialogue options -- special blue or red text
choices that can resolve situations in ways that normal dialogue
cannot.

*Commitment matters.* The game rewards consistency. Spreading your
points across both paths means you may not unlock the highest-tier
Charm or Intimidate options. Pick a path and lean into it, especially
in Mass Effect 2, where critical dialogue checks require high
morality scores.

*Neutral is safe.* Investigate options (left-side) and neutral
responses (middle) do not advance either morality bar. Use these
when you want information without committing to a moral stance.

#callout("tip")[
  Your first playthrough is best experienced by making the choices
  that feel right to you in the moment, not by trying to optimize
  morality points. Save the "perfect run" for a later playthrough.
]

== Exploration and Activities

*Galaxy map.* Between missions, you navigate the galaxy from the
Normandy's galaxy map. Star systems contain planets you can scan
for resources, land on for side missions, or simply admire.

*Planet scanning.* Each game handles resource gathering differently.
In Mass Effect 1, you scan planets from orbit and sometimes land
for mineral deposits. In Mass Effect 2, you scan planets by moving
a cursor and launching probes at resource hotspots. In Mass Effect 3,
scanning is used to locate war assets while avoiding Reaper patrols.
Gather resources regularly -- they fund upgrades and equipment.

*Mako driving (ME1).* The Mako is your all-terrain vehicle for
exploring uncharted worlds. It handles like a shopping cart full
of bricks. Use the jump jets to scale steep terrain. Use the
cannon for ranged combat and the machine gun for close targets.
When fighting Thresher Maws (giant worms), keep moving -- they
cannot hit a moving Mako.

*Side missions first.* Complete available side missions before
advancing the main story. Some side content becomes unavailable
after certain main story beats. If someone says "this is the point
of no return," wrap up your side missions first.

*Hub areas.* The Citadel, Omega, Illium, and other hubs are dense
with vendors, side quests, and ambient conversations. Explore
thoroughly. Check every vendor for upgrades and equipment. Talk to
NPCs with names -- many offer side missions or useful information.

== Equipment and Inventory

*Weapons and armor.* Each character can equip weapons and armor.
Higher-tier equipment (marked with Roman numerals or color rarity)
has better stats. Prioritize damage output for weapons and damage
protection for armor on your Shepard.

*Mods and upgrades.* Weapons and armor accept modification slots.
Common mods include damage barrels, stability dampeners, shield
boosters, and ammo capacity upgrades. Install mods that complement
your playstyle -- a sniper wants damage and stability, a shotgun
user wants melee damage and spare thermal clips.

*Omni-tools and biotic amps.* Tech-focused characters (Engineer,
Infiltrator, Sentinel) benefit from better Omni-tools, which boost
tech power damage and cooldown speed. Biotic characters (Adept,
Vanguard, Sentinel) benefit from better biotic amps, which boost
biotic power damage and duration. Upgrade these when you can.

*Selling versus keeping.* In Mass Effect 1, convert low-tier items
to omni-gel, which is used for bypassing locks and repairing the
Mako. In Mass Effect 2 and 3, sell items you do not need for credits.
Credits fund weapon and armor purchases at vendors.

== New Player Tips

*Save often, in multiple slots.* Cannot stress this enough. Before
every major conversation and every mission, make a manual save.
If you regret a decision or a fight goes badly, you have options.

*Decisions carry across all three games.* Mass Effect imports your
save file from one game to the next. Characters remember what you
did. Some decisions in Mass Effect 1 do not pay off until Mass
Effect 3. Everything matters.

*Talk to your squadmates between every mission.* New dialogue unlocks
after every major mission. Visit every squadmate on the Normandy
after every outing. Their personal stories and loyalty missions are
among the best content in the series.

*Do not skip loyalty missions.* In Mass Effect 2, each squadmate
has a personal loyalty mission. These are not optional side content --
they directly affect outcomes later in the game. Complete every
loyalty mission before the final mission of Mass Effect 2.

*Prepare for the Suicide Mission.* Mass Effect 2's finale is
infamous for a reason. Without spoiling anything: upgrade your
ship fully, complete every loyalty mission, and choose the right
people for the right jobs. Your choices during this mission have
permanent consequences. Save before starting it.

*Enjoy the journey.* Mass Effect is about the people you meet and
the choices you make. The destination matters, but the journey
is why people replay this trilogy for over a decade.
